 \maketitle
 
 \begin{abstract}
-A walking user changes the channel within tens of milliseconds, yet reconfigurable intelligent surface (RIS) phase optimizers are usually compared by the rate they reach on the channel they were given. We instead evaluate each method on the channel that exists when it finishes, its \emph{effective rate}, with measured latencies, in a standardized indoor-office multi-user multiple-input single-output downlink with a 32-element RIS. First, coordinate descent and long-run Adam, the methods with the highest rate on the initial channel, need 0.9--5.9~s and lose to a 2.5~ms single-user heuristic. Second, more iterations help only up to a point: at 1.5~m/s Adam's effective rate peaks at 4--6 steps and running it to 48 steps costs 14.0\%; at 10~km/h the peak is at 3 steps. The optimum is set by the step time relative to the Doppler frequency, as a small-delay model explains. Third, a learned update rule that beats tuned Adam at equal steps is dominated by faster Adam configurations once latency is measured on one processor thread. All results assume the precoder is recomputed from the current channel. For pedestrian users at the measured step cost, latency, not convergence, decides which method is best.
