 \subsection{The Fresh-Beam Assumption Matters}
 \label{sec:res_beam}
-All results assume that the BS refreshes its precoder from the current channel while only the RIS phase ages. On the beam set, keeping the precoder from $t=0$ instead makes the rate collapse within tens of milliseconds: at $T_c$ it is 11.7--14.2$\times$ lower than with a fresh beam, for every phase choice, and with the phase of Adam ($L=8$) a precoder only 5~ms old already lowers the rate from 5.228 to 1.736~b/s/Hz (beam-set rates are not comparable with Table~\ref{tab:aging}). Refreshing it is cheap (0.12~ms, about 13 times shorter than one Adam step), but it requires effective-channel pilots every few milliseconds, whose airtime we do not charge.
+All results assume that the BS refreshes its precoder from the current channel while only the RIS phase ages. On the beam set, keeping the precoder from $t=0$ instead makes the rate collapse within tens of milliseconds: at $T_c$ it is 11.7--14.2$\times$ lower than with a fresh beam, for every phase choice, and with the phase of Adam ($L=8$) a precoder only 5~ms old already lowers the rate from 5.228 to 1.736~b/s/Hz (beam-set rates are not comparable with Table~\ref{tab:aging}). Refreshing it is cheap (0.12~ms, about 13 times shorter than one Adam step), but it requires effective-channel pilots every few milliseconds; Section~\ref{sec:res_robust} charges their airtime.
