+\subsection{Robustness to the Remaining Assumptions}
+\label{sec:res_robust}
+The checks below use the aging set and the re-measured step time of 1.33~ms; where a configuration is selected (precoder period, step size, best method), it is selected on an independent mobility draw, and CIs are paired on the reported one.
+
+\emph{Step time.} With 1.33~ms per step, $L^\star=6$, running to $L=48$ costs 14.9\%, and SU-best-of stays ahead of coordinate descent by 0.26~b/s/Hz (CI 0.15--0.37).
+
+\emph{Precoder pilots.} Let the zero-forcing precoder be refreshed every $R$ ms from four orthogonal pilot symbols that carry no data, with $R$ chosen per method, and average the rate over the 50~ms after deployment. The best rate falls from 5.21 (free refresh) to 3.54, 4.00 and 4.33~b/s/Hz at 15, 30 and 60~kHz spacing, with $R=1$--2~ms; Adam with 3--20 steps remains statistically tied with the best (6--8 steps), and coordinate descent and PGA remain significantly behind. Holding the phase for longer moves the optimum up: averaged over 25, 50 and 100~ms the selected step counts are 5, 6--8 and 16--20, with differences of at most 0.04~b/s/Hz to six steps at 100~ms, consistent with Table~\ref{tab:tracking}.
+
+\emph{Imperfect channel knowledge.} With estimation error of normalized power $1/(1+\rho)$ on all channels, the phase computed from the estimate and the precoder from a fresh estimate, the best effective rate falls from 6.08 to 3.54~b/s/Hz at $\rho=30$~dB; at 10~dB and below the methods cannot be told apart. With pilot airtime charged as well, the selected Adam configuration leads SU-best-of by 0.05--0.06~b/s/Hz at 30~dB and is tied with it at 20~dB, while coordinate descent and PGA stay significantly behind at both levels.
+
+\emph{Discrete phases.} Rounding Adam's phase to 1, 2 or 3 bits keeps an intrinsic optimum ($L^\star=5$, 6 and 6; running to $L=48$ costs 9.5, 14.1 and 14.9\%). Coordinate descent on the discrete grid, timed at 16--501~ms, falls significantly behind the rounded SU-best-of at 3 bits and, with two or more sweeps, at 2 bits; it ties the best rounded Adam only at 1 bit with a single sweep.
+
